<Base section="about" title="About · All Things Banana" description="What All Things Banana is, and how the live banana feed works.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About All Things Banana</h1>
    <p>All Things Banana collects everything banana-related from around the web in one place: banana prices, news, videos and how the big banana companies are doing on the stock market. It also has things to play with in <a href="/more/">More Bananas</a>: the Banana Clicker world leaderboard and a 3D solver for the banana cube puzzle.</p>
    <h2>How the feed works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every 10 minutes we check news and video sites for anything that mentions bananas.</li>
      <li>We filter out things that only share the name, like the clothing brand Banana Republic or the phrase "going bananas".</li>
      <li>Every item links straight to the original source. We show the headline and where it's from, never the full article.</li>
      <li>Stock prices are delayed and are for fun, not financial advice.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Recipes from other sites</h2>
    <p>Peelicious has our own banana recipes, plus links to banana recipes published on other recipe sites. For those, we only show what each site publishes for search engines (the recipe's name, photo, time and rating) and link straight to the original. The method and everything else stays on their site. Our crawler identifies itself as AllThingsBananaBot, follows each site's robots.txt and visits slowly. If you publish recipes and would rather not be listed, let us know and we'll remove them.</p>
    <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Global banana price, and other fruit and soft commodities</strong> (oranges, apples, cocoa, coffee, sugar, tea, rice): the IMF's Primary Commodity Prices, published monthly.</li>
      <li><strong>US shop prices</strong> (bananas, navel oranges, lemons, grapefruit, strawberries): the US Bureau of Labor Statistics' average prices in US cities, monthly since 1995. A few months are missing where the BLS didn't publish, such as during the 2025 government shutdown.</li>
      <li><strong>Records and ranges</strong> cover the data we show, not all of history: banana import prices from 2010, most other series from the 1990s.</li>
      <li><strong>One banana:</strong> the US shop price multiplied by the weight of a medium banana, about 118 g (0.26 lb).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Something slipped through that isn't about bananas, or should we be covering a source we're missing? We'd love to hear about it.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
